  "page": 413,
  "line": 4,
  "situation": "Having appealed away from the judgement seat, he sets one up himself. *But I judge myself · I think upon it again · I pronounce the sentence · I take vengeance myself upon myself*. This is the other half of the appeal at 410: justice was asked to stand aside there, and here the man supplies it in his own person, so that nothing is asked to be waived, only moved. *If we would judge ourselves, we should not be judged* was quoted at printed 386 as a counsel; here it is an act performed — the same verse twice over and never for the same thing. Note the plate cites it there as 1 Cor. xi. 31 and here as xi. 30; the volume gives its own right figure, and both stand as printed.",
  "source": "identified",
  "band": "R"
 },
